comment_html:
Absolutely the most incredible hiking experience of my life. The scramble at the top is not easy, especially if you're relatively new to scrambling (which I am). The scramble is not technically difficult (probably on the easier side of things, and there is no "falling to your death" fear) but it requires a good level of fitness and significant effort. But the feeling at the top is indescribable, both because of where you end up (the view) and because of the sense of accomplishment it took to get there.


Side note - every pic and video I saw of the a Crack trail involved dozens and dozens of people. I avoided the crowds by arriving and starting the trail right at the "Crack" of dawn. I had the entire trail to myself, and I was alone at the top for more than 45 minutes before I bagan my decent. As I was climbing down, I literally passed more than 100 people on their way up. I was very glad to have missed all of them. It is a very popular trail, just loaded with people, but you can have it to yourself with a little planning. I'd never hike it any other way.&nbsp;


While it said that the trail is well marked, because I was alone and didn't have 40 people in front of me to follow, I actually had a trouble finding the next marker at times, especially once the trail gets into the open rocks. I made it to one marker, and could see no discernable path, nor the next marker... This happened two or three times, and while I eventually found the right way every time, it took a bit of (fun) exploring. Nevertheless, I dispute that this trail is particularly well marked, though it is definitely well maintained.&nbsp;

